course: medicine
lesson: 15
title: Eczema and other skin troubles
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Healthy skin is like a brick wall that keeps water in and irritants (things that make skin sore) out; in eczema the wall is leaky, so the skin dries out, cracks and itches.
explain: Scratching damages the skin more, which makes it itch more: the itch-scratch cycle, worst at night.
explain: Thick moisturisers called emollients every day, and steroid creams for flare-ups used as the doctor says, keep eczema calm.
explain: Eczema, psoriasis, birthmarks and warts all look different, but eczema, psoriasis and birthmarks are not catching at all.
explain: Nobody did anything wrong to get eczema; it often runs in families together with asthma and hay fever.
fact: In eczema the skin barrier doesn't hold water well, so the skin dries, cracks and lets irritants in.
fact: Eczema often runs in families together with asthma and hay fever, and it is not catching or caused by dirtiness.
fact: Doctors treat eczema with emollients every day and steroid creams for flare-ups, the times when it gets worse.
fact: Scratching damages the skin so it itches more, the itch-scratch cycle; short nails and cotton clothes help.
fact: Steroid creams used as the doctor says are safe and don't thin the skin when used properly.
fact: Broken eczema skin can be infected by bacteria or the cold sore virus; crusty, weepy patches need a GP, and sudden painful blisters with fever need urgent help.
fact: Cold wind and dry heating take water from the skin; licking your lips makes them more chapped, and lip balm helps.
fact: Psoriasis is when skin cells grow too fast and build up in thick scaly patches; it is linked to the immune system and is not catching.
fact: Birthmarks are made by extra blood vessels or pigment (skin colour), are mostly harmless, and are not caused by anything the mother did.
fact: Warts are small rough lumps caused by a virus; many go away by themselves within months to two years, and toads don't give you warts.
fact: Contact dermatitis is a skin reaction to something it touches, like perfumed soap, plants or metals; a mild soap-free wash is kinder to skin that reacts easily.
exercise: Why does eczema skin get dry and itchy? | Its barrier doesn't hold water well | It has far too much water trapped inside it | It is full of germs from dirt | It has been sunburnt over and over
exercise: What is the itch-scratch cycle? | Scratching damages skin so it itches even more | Itching that comes back every Tuesday | Scratching hard enough that it cures the itch for good | An itch that moves around the body
exercise: What helps stop night scratching? | Short nails and cotton clothes or gloves | Thick wool pyjamas buttoned up to the neck | Long nails | Hot baths every hour of the night
exercise: Can you catch eczema by holding hands? | No, eczema is not catching | Yes, by touching the dry patches | Yes, by sharing pens and pencils | Only from someone in your own family
exercise: What are emollients? | Thick moisturisers used every day | Antibiotic tablets for the skin | A kind of strong soap | Special plasters for eczema patches
exercise: Are steroid creams safe for eczema? | Yes, when used as the doctor says | No, they always thin the skin badly | Yes, and the more you use the better | No, they are banned for children
exercise: Your eczema turned crusty and weepy. What might have happened? | It may be infected and needs a GP | It is healing fast and will drop off soon | It has turned into psoriasis | It's just sweat drying on it
exercise: Why is your face chapped in winter? | Cold wind and dry heating take water from the skin | You ate too much ice cream | Winter air is full of tiny germs that eat away at your skin | Snow is acidic and burns it
exercise: Does licking your lips help chapped lips? | No, it makes them more chapped | Yes, spit is a good moisturiser | Yes, ten times an hour helps | No, but biting the dry bits off does
exercise: What is psoriasis? | Skin cells growing too fast into scaly patches | A catching fungus picked up at the pool | A bad sunburn | A kind of eczema that comes from dirt
exercise: What makes a birthmark? | Extra blood vessels or pigment in the skin | Something the mother ate while she was pregnant | A bump before birth | Sunburn the baby got in the womb
exercise: What causes a wart? | A virus in the top layer of skin | Touching a toad or a frog by the pond | Eating far too many greasy chips | Biting your nails, and only that
exercise: Why did your skin go red and bumpy after a new soap? | The skin reacted to something in the soap | The soap was too cheap to be any good | You washed too little | The water was far too cold for the soap to wash off properly
exercise: Did you do something wrong to get eczema? | No, it often runs in families | Yes, by not washing often enough | Yes, by eating too many sweets | Yes, by touching a cat
exercise: Do most warts need treatment? | No, many go away by themselves | Yes, every wart needs a small operation | Yes, they keep growing for ever if left | No, but they spread to everyone you touch
test: dad's got psoriasis, mate says don't touch him or you'll catch it. true? | No, psoriasis is not catching | Yeah, it spreads by touching his skin | Yeah, but only to kids, not to adults | No, but you catch eczema from it
test: In a fairy tale, a witch says touching a toad gives you warts. What's true? | Warts are caused by a virus, not toads | Toads do give you warts if you hold them | Frogs give warts, toads don't | Warts come from kissing frogs
test: Mum's scared to use my steroid cream in case it thins my skin. What would the doctor say? | Used as directed, it's safe and doesn't thin the skin | She's right, never use it | Use the whole tube at once to get it over with | Only use it on the healthy skin round the patch
test: My friend won't hold hands in the circle because of his eczema. How can we help? | Be kind and treat it as normal | Tease him until he gives in and does it | Tell everyone it's catching | Make him wear thick gloves all day
test: Grandma says my friend's birthmark is because his mum ate strawberries. Right? | No, birthmarks are not caused by anything the mother did | Yes, strawberries cause them | Yes, eating lots of red food when pregnant does it | No, it's from sunburn
test: What would happen if you stopped using emollients every day? | Eczema skin could dry out and flare up | The eczema would be cured for good, as the skin learns to cope | The skin would get far too oily | Nothing at all
test: Ellie's eczema suddenly has painful blisters and she has a fever. What now? | Get urgent medical help | Scratch the blisters open to let it out | Wait a month | Put more soap on to clean it
test: I lick my lips all winter cos they're so dry. Clever? | No, licking makes them more chapped; use lip balm | Yes, spit is a moisturiser | Yes, and bite them too | No, rub some salt on them instead
test: Which is the best soap for skin that reacts easily? | A mild soap-free wash | The most expensive perfumed soap in the shop | Washing-up liquid, as it's gentle on hands | Any soap with glitter in it
test: Why are eczema and asthma often in the same family? | They share an extra sensitive immune system that runs in families | They are caught from each other when one of them coughs | Families share towels | They are the same disease in two places
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises eczema? | A condition of impaired skin barrier, neither contagious nor caused by uncleanliness | A contagious infection transmitted by touch | A consequence of inadequate washing and poor hygiene | An infection acquired by handling toads
exam: Identify the principal daily treatment for eczema. | Regular application of emollients | Daily application of perfumed soap to the affected skin | Avoidance of all contact with water | Daily courses of antibiotics
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning warts. | They are caused by a virus and frequently resolve unaided | They are acquired by handling toads | They require surgical removal in every case, without exception | They are caused by the consumption of fried food
exam: State the effect of repeatedly licking chapped lips. | The chapping is aggravated | The lips are moisturised and healed | The lips are protected from cold wind | The chapping is entirely prevented by saliva
